{
  "id": "KY-HB-44-2024",
  "scope": "state:ky",
  "title": "HB 44 (2024 Regular Session): AN ACT relating to elections.",
  "urls": [
    "https://apps.legislature.ky.gov/record/24RS/hb44.html",
    "https://apps.legislature.ky.gov/record/24RS/hb44/vote_history.pdf"
  ],
  "version": "enrolled",
  "operative_text": "(a_in_person_id) not stated.\n(b_no_id_remedy) not stated.\n(c_mail_ballot_id) not stated.\n(d_proof_of_citizenship)\n(section) Sec. 1 (KRS 30A.145(2))\n(quote) The Administrative Office of the Courts shall prepare, on the first Tuesday of each month, a list of all persons who were excused from jury duty for not being a citizen of the United States and provide the list to the Attorney General, the United States Attorney of the appropriate jurisdiction, and the State Board of Elections.\n(section) Sec. 4 (KRS 116.113(3))\n(quote) Upon receipt of notification from the Administrative Office of the Courts that a person has been excused from jury duty for not being a citizen of the United States, the State Board of Elections shall within five (5) days cause the removal of the name of that person from the voter registration records it maintains, except that no voter's name may be removed during the period of time the registration books are closed for any primary, general, or special election.\n(section) Sec. 3 (KRS 116.0452(3)(b))\n(quote) As provided by KRS 116.113, upon notice of death, declaration of incompetency, excusal from jury duty for not being a United States citizen, or conviction of a felony;\n(e_other_rule)\n(section) Sec. 2(1)(c) (new section of KRS ch. 116)\n(quote) The number of voter eligibility changes, which shall include relocation out of state, death, incarceration, expungement, and citizenship status changes;\n(section) Sec. 2(1)(d)\n(quote) Any use of a national voter registration clearinghouse system or interstate agreement to remove inaccurate or fraudulent registrations and the cost of those agreements.\n(other) Sec. 1: KRS 30A.145 - circuit clerk still sends notices of incompetency; the Administrative Office of the Courts now makes the monthly citizenship-excusal list.\n(other) Sec. 2 (new, KRS ch. 116): yearly voter-registration cleanup report by the Secretary of State with the State Board of Elections, due July 1, sent to the Legislative Research Commission and posted online; contents listed.\n(other) Sec. 2(1)(d): no contract after April 1, 2025 may bind the state to spend money or effort to register unregistered people; data-exchange agreements to remove ineligible voters are allowed.\n(other) Sec. 2(2): State Board of Elections yearly address report (voters per address, personal information redacted, people at those addresses contacted only by mail); public access at county clerk and online; public link to report anomalies and a form for a voter to cancel his or her own registration.\n(other) Sec. 3: KRS 116.0452 - restates when a registration is timely and when a name may be removed; adds citizenship excusal to the removal grounds by reference.\n(other) Sec. 4: KRS 116.113 - Cabinet for Health and Family Services gives lifetime death records to the Board by July 1 each year; Board removes names within 5 days on notice of death, incompetency, citizenship excusal, felony, or out-of-state registration; renumbers subsections.\n(other) Sec. 5 (new, KRS ch. 117): bans ranked-choice voting (ranking candidates, multi-round tabulation) for any local, state or federal elective office; conflicting local ordinances void.\n(other) version: orig_bill (title 'relating to voter registration'): citizenship rule in slot d already present in Sec. 1 and Sec. 4, but the monthly list is made by each circuit clerk, goes to the Administrative Office of the Courts, which reports within 10 days to the Attorney General, U.S. Attorney and State Board. Report and address-report due dates are February 1. No ranked-choice section. No slots a-c, e beyond the same eligibility-changes line.\n(other) version: 0177: the Acts Chapter 177 printing (enrolled; 'Veto Overridden April 12, 2024'). Title 'relating to elections'. Citizenship list now made by the Administrative Office of the Courts and sent straight to the Attorney General, U.S. Attorney and State Board; dates moved to July 1; adds the April 1, 2025 contract limit; adds the new ranked-choice ban (Sec. 5); drops the address-report purpose clause 'to verify that his or her address is not being used fraudulently by other registered voters'. Slot d removal rule (KRS 116.113(3)) unchanged from orig_bill.\n(other) version: bill: furthest version; the vetoed-and-overridden text. Word for word the same as 0177 (only the 'ACTS OF THE GENERAL ASSEMBLY' line differs). It also equals SCS 1 plus the SCA 1 title change; HCS 1, SCS 1 and SCA 1 are now saved (see lines below).\n(other) version: HCS1 (House committee substitute 1, 24 RS HB 44/HCS 1, saved 2026-11-11): carries slot d (citizenship list check) and the slot e eligibility-changes line. Against the introduced bill: the Administrative Office of the Courts, not each circuit clerk, prepares the monthly jury-excusal list and sends it on (no 10-day relay through a clerk); a limit on contracts after April 1, 2025; report due dates move from February to July; the address-report purpose clause 'to verify that his or her address is not being used fraudulently by other registered voters' is dropped. The removal rule in KRS 116.113(3) is unchanged.\n(other) version: SCS1 (Senate committee substitute 1, 24 RS HB 44/SCS 1, saved 2026-11-11): same slot d and e text as HCS 1, word for word apart from the header. Its one substantive change is a new Section 5 creating a ban on ranked-choice voting (KRS ch. 117).\n(other) version: SCA1 (Senate committee amendment 1-title, saved 2026-11-11): title only. It changes the title to 'AN ACT relating to elections.' and has no ID or citizenship text. The saved 'bill' and 0177 equal SCS 1 plus this title.\n(other) note: Slot d: the citizenship rule is a list check, not a proof requirement. People excused from jury duty for not being U.S. citizens are put on a monthly court list; the State Board must remove those people from the voter rolls within five days. It reaches only people the court list names, not every voter and not new registrants. It does not touch ID rules (slots a-c) and no documentary proof of citizenship is required. The same bill also bans ranked-choice voting. The actions show the Governor's veto was overridden on 2024-04-12 (the veto message itself is not in the files); the files do not give the Governor's reasons. The House committee substitute 1 and the Senate committee substitute 1 and committee amendment (1-title) are not saved; floor amendments 1-3 withdrawn and 4 defeated in the Senate per the actions. 0177 and bill carry identical words, so I treat them as the final text. The final act uses July 1 dates (orig_bill had February 1).\n(note) Off this topic: the bill sets no ID or citizenship-proof rule.",
